NB: There are numerous values and formulas in this document.  Each of them is
a rough, early-design representation.  Any value used in this document is
subject to scaling during balancing.  Text that was added early on talks about
scale factors in some cases.  After a while, I stopped mentioning them, since
I now consider tham to be implicit.


The map, and colonization:
==========================

* The map is comprised of hexes.  Each hex contains multiple systems.

* Each system may have multiple important locations within it.  Such locations
  include settlements, starbases, outposts, etc.  Each such location may have
  multiple things in it.  For instance: the homeworld and the starting outpost
  are in the same system location.

* Each hex has the smae number of systems, and those systems are discovered
  when you enter an adjacent hex.  The lore is that these are the only systems
  to which you can navigate using subspace.

* Each hex has multiple habitable planets within it.  They must be discovered
  via the Explore fleet mission.

* Settlements have a single value that represents their level of population,
  infrastructure, etc., "development".  1 development roughly corresponds to 1
  million people (1 "pop") on a highly developed world.

* Hexes are colonized a planet at a time, using reusable colony ships.  Colony
  ships require resources to operate.  Colony ships can develop existing
  settlements (deliver development), or start new ones.  It takes more
  resources (maybe longer) to start new settlements.  Lifters can be used to
  deliver development to a settlement too, at a reduced effectiveness (say,
  half).

* Settlements consume resources in the early turns, then develop into
  resource-producers.  The threshold between needing resources and producing
  them is I1.

* A planet can contain multiple settlements from multiple nations.  Nations
  can attack and/or force other nations' settlements to move.  Each nation may
  or may not know about other nations' settlements.

* Each province of hexes earns you extra income from the planets in that
  province, and makes settlements cheaper within the province.  A province is
  6 connected hexes.

* Forming a province takes resources.

* The home hex for each nation starts with multiple worlds, an outpost, a
  reasonably sized fleet of weak ships, and a colony ship.


Planets:
========

* Planets have resources in different degrees af availability.  Metal, energy
  (may be anything from fossil fuels to hydro), (ship) fuel mineral (the raw
  ingredients for ship fuel), water, food.  The availability of these are on a
  point scale, 0-100.  The actual amount produced is this factor, times the
  infrastructure score [0.0, 1.0], when fully staffed with enough pops.

* There are some properties that make a planet more or less suitable.
  Different tech levels may mitigate some of the malus-inducing properties.
  Some ideas here are: (-) abundant heavy metals, (-) left-chiral life, (-)
  hostile fauna, (-) hostile flora, (-) high rotational tilt, (-) lack of
  water, (+) low rotational tilt, (+) domesticable local species.


Supply:
=======

* Supply starts in each system that that both produces supply and that has
  supply throughput (which comes from available transports and bases+infra),
  and goes outward from there S1 world units.  If supply encounters a mobile
  base, outpost, starbase, or other supply-exporting system (owned by the same
  nation) supply radiates out from that system, etc.  Stated another way, a
  system is in supply if it can trace a path from itself to a supplying
  system, from supply node to supply node, without ever moving more than S1.
  Note that the amount of supply they receive depends upon supply throughput.

* Supply may be disrupted by enemy actin, including destroying or capturing
  transports.

* One unit of food is enough to feed one pop (1M people) for one month.
  Taking a "typical" crew size of 1k, it should then require 0.001 food to
  feed one ship for one month.  Same thing for one unit of water.  There are
  replacement parts needed for ships, ordnance, and fighters.  Those all
  require metals, (ground) energy, and money.  A ship requires 1 unit of fuel
  per hull size per engine size.


Ships:
======

* Ships should be relatively plentiful.

* Each ship has a combat factor.  The earliest ships will have a factor of 10
  or 20.  Large ships in the mid-game will have factors of 70-80.  Ships in the
  late game will have factors mich higher, perhaps up to 150 or 200.  The combat
  factor is the base value used for construction, shield, and weapons.  Some
  ships will have higher a defense factor than attack factor (e.g. carrier
  escorts), and others will have only a defense factor (e.g. colony ships and
  lifters).

* Some ships carry fighers.  Each fighter factor is a combat factor of 10.
  Each fighter factor may represent more than one actual fighter.

* Non-combat operations (delivering ground forces, setting up outposts and
  bases, etc.) must be done by special craft, "lifters".  Lifters have large
  stealth and propulsion maluses, like the largest warships.  They have no
  attack factor, but have high defense factors.

* Most ships should travel at the same speed: S1 world units per month.  S1
  should depend on the current tech level.  The lightest ships should get a
  bonus on top of S1.  The heaviest should get a malus.  This may apply to
  heavy warships, but must definitely apply to lifters, colony ships, and
  transports.

* When the tech level goes up, a ship must be upgraded to get the new speed
  S1.  They can do this at a starbase (limit SB1 ships/month), or at the
  shipyard (much higher limits).  The upgrades cost resources.

* Each ship's tech level in each tech area is listed, along with the current
  tech level.  For instance, detection may be listed as 2/5 if the ship's
  detection hardware is level 2, and level 5 has been researched.

* When ships are damaged in combat, they must be repaired at a mobile repair
  yard (limited to P1 points/month), or the shipyard (many more repair points,
  but taking away from production capacity).  The repairs cost money.

* Each nation has a ship build schedule; builds are at the main shipyard in
  the home system.  It is *extremely* expensive to establish new shipyards,
  including if/when the old one is destroyed.  All ships in the schedule may
  be built at face value.  Any ships in excess of that may only be built in
  times of war, and for double price.

* The schedule includes a mix of small, medium, and large warships.  It also
  includes lifters, colony ships, and transports.

* When a new ship design N is created, that design must be added to the
  shipyard.  Lets say the old design O is very different.  Then adding the new
  design would be like adding a new production line from scratch.  If the
  designs are very similar, the cost (TODO or maybe loss in build speed?) is
  low-to-no.

* Shipyards appear in the same system location as the starting outpost and
  homeworld.

* The smallest ships have a bonuses to their detection, stealth, and
  propulsion ratings.  The largest have maluses.

* Transports should exist, and should be very soft targets.  You should have
  to produce them for resources just like other ships, a la HOI4.

* Ship cost is:
  - Hull cost (by volume, maybe) +
  - Armor cost +
  - Propulsion cost +
  - Weapons cost +
  - Shields cost +
  - Detection equipment cost +
  - Stealth cost

* TODO: Reliability!  It should be expressed as a fuzzy number to the user (or
  maybe not at all?), but should represent the monthly chance that a single
  point in the damage table will be destroyed at random during non-combat
  operation.

* TODO: Possibly allow the automation level to be part of the design.  Lower
  automation means more crew, and higher automation means less crew.  Higher
  automation costs more materials and money though.

* Ship mass is hull + armor.

* A ship design is made by picking the tech level for each stat (armor 1
  vs. armor 2, etc.).  The total cost would be in all the resources involved.
  Obviously, not all ships need all those elements (stealth, detection, and
  shields for instance).  Also, the latest available components need not be
  used.  Older stuff should be cheaper.  Quantity over quality should be a
  viable strategy at times (or for your whole run).

* 5 crew are requred for each point of hull.  An additional 100 crew are
  required per point of equipment, far all these kinds of equipment:
  Propulsion, Weapons, Shields, Detection, Stealth and Fighters.  TODO: Can
  you add an increment of 100 troops as marines, to board enemy ships, or
  defend against boarding?

* Hull space is occupied by the drive, weapons, shields, and stealth tech, at
  10 points per level of each equipped item.  So a design with these stats
  (hull=100, propulsion=2, weapons=2, shields=2, stealth=1) would have 30
  space left over after 20+20+20+10=70 space is used for all the listed
  equipment.  3 points of space is required per 1k crew (quarters, life
  support, whatever).  Assuming a 1k crew size, that leaves 29 space for fuel,
  ordnance, water, supplies, and general cargo/troops.

* 1 unit of ship space is enough room for: 1000 people and their equipment,
  which may be 1000 colonists, or 1000 troops, etc.; 1 unit of fuel, which is
  enough fuel for a mass-100 ship to burn for one month in subspace, or for a
  mass-100 ship to burn for one day at full accelaration (at starting tech
  levels); 1 unit of supply or water, which is enough to eat or drink for 1k
  crew for one month; 1 unit of any resource as cargo; 10 shots; or 10
  missiles.  Taking the 29 units of leftover space from before, 2 units each
  of fuel, water, and supply would give the ship 2 months of operating time,
  plus a small buffer.  That leaves 23.  1 point each of rounds and missiles
  would leave the ship with 21 units of space for general cargo, or just empty
  space.  A high-quality design will use cargo and empty space as buffers
  around ammo and fuel.

* Tech advances should: reduce the space requirements for a particular level
  of equipment; lower the mass of a point of hull; lower the mass of a point
  of armor; and reduce the fuel cost per unit of distance traveled (or per
  unit of time spent accelerating in-system).  NB: This implies that physics
  works like we expect in normal space -- you accelerate to move in acertain
  direction.  However, in subspace, the rules are different; you constantly
  expend fuel at a certain rate to move at a constant rate of speed.

* Designs are rolled, a la Shadow Empire.  Given T, the tech level chosen for
  a particular stat (say, armor tech 3), the base stat in the ship design is T
  * 10 (or design armor 30 in this case).  The initial design is created from
  the base stats, but with some random values subtracted from the baseline
  values indicated by the tech levels.  Military tradition mitigates the
  magnitude of the amount subtracted.  (Similarly, an amount may be added to
  the space required for each component.)  After having used the design for a
  while, you should gain design-relevant knowledge that allows you to roll a
  new iteration of the design; the new design will benefit from the use of the
  previous design, proportional to the amount learned.  Design points are
  acumulated via combat for combat-related stats (including hull), successful
  hiding/detection for those stats, and flying around for the propulsion stat.
  Building ships should make the money/resource costs, build time, and
  reliability rolls improve.

* Design upgrades cannot change the hull size or the propulsion tech.

* Subspace (interstellar) speed is based purely on the propulsion equipment
  level, P.  The ship will move P world units each month.  In-system ship
  acceleration Asys is propulsion / mass; system travel times are based on
  that, with an upper limit of 1.5G for sustained acceleration.  At higher
  levels of propulsion+shield+construction techs, this upper limit is raised,
  due to the invention of artificial gravity.

NB: Distance formula for linear acceleration:
d = 0.5 * a * pow(t, 2)

NB: Time to travel distance d at accceleration a:
t = sqrt(2 * d / a)

NB: Time to travel distance d at acceleration a, with a flip and deceleration
at the midpoint:
t = sqrt(d / a) + sqrt(d / a) = 2 * sqrt(d / a)

* A ship attack works like this.  For ships 1 and 2, W1 (ship 1's weapons
  rating) is the base damage.  This attack consumes one round and one missile.
  If the defending ship has at least one round remaining, it fires it as a PD
  salvo, and has a chance to destroy the incoming missile.  The success
  probability is 0.25 * (1 + W2 - W1 + Asys2 - Asys1).

* When a hit occurs, the damage Ds through the shields is W1 - S2 (or W1 / 2 -
  S2 that if the missile was shot down).  This damages the ship like this.
  The damage to the armor is Da = Ds * A2_curr / A2_full.  The damage to the
  hull is Ds - Da.  There is a damage table of in-hull locations that can be
  damaged.  The table includes the space occupied by each equipment part
  (weapons, drive, etc.), and points for the occupancy of all the other space
  (fuel, cargo, supplies, etc.).  Each point of hull damage has a chance to
  damage one slot in the damage table.  The first point has a 1 / H2 chance to
  damage one point from the listed stats.  The second has a 2 / H2 chance, and
  so on.  If a piece of ship equipment is damaged, its performance is degraded
  accordingly.  If fuel is hit, the locations in the damage table on either
  side are also hit; this could cause a chain reaction.  If ammo is hit, the
  same thing happens, except that it is the two locations on either side that
  are also hit.  Better designs will place fuel and ammo in isolated slots,
  not near critical equipment; worse designs will have them wherever.

* TODO: How do hits cause crew casualties?  There should be more crew
  casualties when taking hits at lower org.

* If any hit destroys the last remaining fuel or point of propulsion, the ship
  stops participating in combat.  Otherwise, it keeps participating as best it
  can, given its remaining stats, ammo, etc.

* After combat, any ship that has no remaining supplies, fuel, or ship-used
  water, is scuttled; having water in cargo is not sufficient.

* In fleet battles, ships are divided up into captials and screens.  Every
  ship below the mean Asys for the fleet is a screen; all others are capitals.
  Combat is comprised of rounds.  Each round, each ship chooses a target to
  attack.  The selected target is chosen at random from among the opposing
  ships, except that each screen is represented twice.  Carriers with Ne
  dedicated escorts are not attacked as part of the screens-vs-capitals target
  selection.  Instead, the carrier and its escorts are all counted once, like
  capital ships.  If the carrier itself gets selected for attack, a new roll
  is done, [0, Ne]; the resulting carrier or escort gets attacked.  NB:
  Ignoring the carrier group for a moment, this means that in a fleet with 10
  capitals and 20 escorts, only 1 in 5 attacks will be directed toward a
  capital.  TODO: Allow non-carrer capitals to get dedicated escorts too?
  TODO: Are cloaked ships that attack allowed to go around these rules
  somehow?

* After a fleet ends its travel, it requires a certain number of days of
  cooldown before it can travel again.  This should change based on tech.
  Consider having interdictor ships that increase this time, scaled by their
  proximity to the affected ships.  Also, a ship should be required to be at
  the edge of a system (far from its star, perhaps based on stellar masses),
  in the direction of the new system it wants to go to.  Ships should not be
  able to travel to interstellar space.  The masses of stars interfere with
  subspace drives when they are too close, but are also necessary anchors for
  subspace travel.  A fleet can do an emergency departure (presumably to avoid
  getting destroyed), when they have not reached the end of the cooldown
  timer, or are too close to the star in their current system.  This
  randomizes the place they end up at the end of their travel.  Friendlies
  (and enemies) cannot come to help or attack them.  If they have fuel and
  supplies enough, they can do a normal travel to a system after the cooldown.
  Doing an emergency jump at that point should have dire consequences.  If
  they run out of supplies, they might lose crew (and thus operational
  capabilities).  If they lack fuel, they may end up stranded.

* Bases at planets should be able to be covered by planetary defenses.  If the
  base is in LPO (like "LEO", but generic), the defenses should "come around"
  every few months, and fire at enemies.  If the base is a GSO, only the
  defenses on that side of the planet should be able to fire.  GSO bases can
  be supported then by numerous planetary batteries, and are relatively safer.
  LPO bases are cheaper (TODO: and maybe cheaper to build at?).


Other units:
============

* Mobile bases must be built.  They are part of the build schedule.  They can
  be over-built (for 2x cost, but only during war, as always).

* Mobile bases must be towed via lifter out to a location.  They are not
  operational until they have been assembled.  This requires the lifter.

* A lifter is required to upgrade a mobile base to an outpost.  This takes
  time; a second lifter can be used to speed things up.  This upgrade takes
  resources and money.

* A lifter is also required to upgrade an outpost to a starbase.  This takes
  time; up to two additional lifters can be used to speed things up.  This
  upgrade takes resources and money.

* Starbases are always in full supply, and can manufacture their own ordnance
  and fighters.  Mobile bases and outposts may or may not be in supply.  This
  manufacturing capability is independent of the ones for settlements.  It
  does not require pops, but should be modest in its capacity.

* Mobile bases, outposts, and starbases are each supply nodes.  They have
  varying degrees of stockpile capcity and supply throughout, depending on the
  details of their design.  Higher-tech designs will have more; starbases will
  have more than outposts, which will in turn have more than mobile bases.

* Ouposts and starbases have fighters (past a certain tech level).  At higher
  tech levels, fighters can be used anywhere in the system of the base; at
  even higher tech levels, they can move into nearby systems, moving like
  fleets.

* Ground units are called divisions.  They exist to protect worlds; they have
  fighters.  Their fighters can only be used on the planet on which they are
  based, or, at later tech levels, in the system in which they are based.

* Mobile repair yards exist to repair and upgrade ships in the field, so that
  they don't have to return to a starbase or the shipyard.  They must be built
  specially, and towed with a lifter like a mobile base.  They may only be
  built during times of war.


Fleets and missions:
====================

* Ships can be organized into fleets.

* Each ship has an organization rating [0, 1.0].  This is overall battle
  readiness (which includes morale).  This value is multiplied by the raw
  combat factors of the ship to get the effective values for each factor.

* Each ship has an XP rating [-1.0, 1.0].  For each combat factor F, F is
  modified by ship_xp * ship_xp_impact * F.  So, if ship_xp_impact is 0.5, a
  top-XP unit would have 150% of its combat factors, and a bottom-XP crew
  (i.e. that wasn't even fully trained) would have 50% of its combat factors.

* Replacing crew due to attrition, servicemembers leaving the service, etc.,
  will affect org and xp, proportional to the percentage of crew replaced.
  Org eventually recovers; XP does not.  Note that this implies that having
  longer service requirements keeps ship crew XPs up.

* Each fleet has an fleet XP rating [0, 1.0].  Each fleet gets a bonus to each
  ship's effective XP rating equal to fleet_xp * fleet_xp_impact.  This
  represents their ability to work together as a single force.  Replacing
  ships proprtionately affects fleet XP.

* Fleets have an engagement posture setting, [0, 100].  It ranges from run
  away no matter what (0), to fight no matter what (100).  The settings in
  between are based on estimated chances of winning.  A setting of 50 would
  mean that the fleet should take 50-50 fights.

* Fleets will return to the nearest supply node to get more supply, water,
  fuel, and ordnance.  They will do this when they are down to the lowest of
  supply, water, or fuel that it would take to get back to resupply, plus a
  configurable margin [0, 0.25].  Independently, they will return when down to
  a configurable amount of remaining ordnance [0, 0.9].  Transports can be
  assigned to fleets, which will increase their available amounts of all these
  quantities, but the transports are also vulnerable to attack.

* Fleets have repair settings as well.  Fleets will notify the user that they
  need repairs, and ask which repair facility they should move to, unless they
  have auto-detatch enabled.  Auto-detach will break off detachments of just
  the ships that need repairs.  Repair detachments start with the fleet XP of
  their original fleet, but slowly lose fleet XP.  They stop losing it when
  they are reunited with their original fleet; over time, they will regain
  half of what they lost.

* Fleets can have several missions.  TODO: Privateer, Pirate, Disrupt
  embargoed trade, Disrupt nonallied trade, ...?

* Idle: Hold position.  This is the default.

* Exercise: Conduct force-on-force exercises, slowly increasing
  crew XP, but dramatically lowering org.  Exercises cost money and use
  consumables like fuel and ordnance.

* Explore: Fly about fro system to system and survey the planets in each, in
  order to find habitable planets.  Better detection and propulsion make this
  work better.

* Hide: Try to remain undetected, and try not to fight units that enter the
  target system.  The fleet must remain undetected for this to work.  The
  detection chance increases month by month for uncloaked ships.  The fleet
  may try to hide behind a large outer planet in the system.

* Intercept in system: the fleet should move to intercept ships moving into or
  through the target system.  It will intercept them as far away as possible,
  given the detection done by all friendlies.  This makes it a good idea to
  have a picket line of small ships throughout the system you want to defend.

* Protect system location: the fleet will move to intercept ships moving
  toward the target location.  It will intercept them only when they get
  reasonably near.

* Hunt enemy fleets: the fleet will move to likely engagement locations, and
  engage enemies.

* Guard supply: the fleet will guard supply transports.

* Attack settlement defenses: Attack garrisons and any surface-to-space
  planetary defenses in a settlement.  May only be done when at war with the
  target settlement's nation.  There is a danger of attacking other nations'
  settlements by mistake.

* Attack settlement infrastructure: Reduce infra on the target settlement.  May
  only be done when at war with the target settlement's nation.  There is a
  danger of attacking other nations' settlements by mistake.

* Attack settlement population: Kill pops in the target settlement.  May only
  be done when at war with the target settlement's nation.  There is a danger
  of attacking other nations' settlements by mistake.  This is considered a
  war crime.

* Blockade settlement: Capture or destroy all transports coming to or from the
  target settlement.  There is a very high danger of captiring and/or
  destroying other nations' transports by mistake.


Special missions:
=================

* Fleeets which contain colony ships have additonal missions.

* Found settlement: Start a settlement on the target planet.  Takes time,
  during which the colony ship must stay at the planet.

* Expand settlement: Increase the speed of infra construction at the target
  settlement.  This implies a proportional increase in consumption of money
  and resources.  This massively increase the drain on shipboard qunatities,
  which will require the colony ship to have to return to a supply point for
  resupply.  Assigning transports to the colony fleet mitigates this.

* Fleeets which contain lifters have additonal missions.

* Deliver divisions: Deliver garrison divisions to target settlement; requires
  setup time.

* Deliver mobile repair yard: Requires setup time.

* Deliver mobile base: Requires setup time.

* Build outpost: Requires build time.

* Build starbase: Requires build time.

* Upgrade base: Upgrades the design; requires build time.

* Expand settlement: [See colony ships.]


Resources, maintenance, industry, and tech:
===========================================

* Resources are produced by settlements.

* Resources can be stockpiled, lent out, given to, and/or borrowed from, other
  nations.  Lending and borrowing imply interest.  Inability to repay loans
  may result in bankruptcy.

* Maintenance must be paid for ships, divisions, mobile bases, and outposts.
  If maintenance is M%, the combat factors for an affected unit are scaled by
  M%.  TODO: Consider allowing bases to be "turned off".  TODO: Consider an
  N-month lag between increasing maintenance/turning maintenance back on and
  full increase of combat strength.

* Maintenance (money and supply) must be paid for settlements before they
  reach I1 development.

* Shipyards produce ships and other units (except garrisons).  To build
  everything in the build schedule, the right amount of resources, money, and
  pops must be supplied.  Provide less of any of those, and everything cannot
  be built.  Supply more resources if you want to over-build.  Overbuilds are
  2x resource and money cost, and do not require more pops.

* Resources and money may also be spent to upgrade the shipyard.  This is
  effectively an increase to the build schedule.

* Tech consumes resources as well.  Spending on tech increases the per-month
  chance of developing a given tech.  The chance starts at 0%, but maxes out
  at 94%

* Kinds of resources: money, metal, (planetarily-used) energy, (ship) fuel
  minerals, water, food.  Energy may be needed in combination with one or more
  other resources in order to make ship components and other manufactured
  goods.

* Refining ship fuel should be done at space refineries, which are subject to
  attack.  Planetside refineries should be the startgin default, but will
  produce way less fuel.  After a certain tech, space refineries become
  available.


Tech:
=====

* There are multiple areas of tech: stealth, detection, construction,
  propulsion, weapons, shields, and automation

* Stealth allows your ships to avoid detection.

* Detection allows you to condict planetary surveys more quickly; detect
  cloaked ships and uncloaked ships; and the presence and location of other
  nations' settlements.

* Construction allows you to build larger and tougher ships and bases.

* Propulsion lets you build faster ships, by increasing your S1.

* Weapons allows you to build more deadly ships and bases.

* Shields allows you to build tougher ships and bases.

* Automation reduces the crewpower needed to crew ships; reduces ship build
  time and monetary cost (and resource cost to a lesser extent); reduces
  infrastucture build/repair time; and reduces the time to research other
  techs (except automation).

* At higher levels of stealth+propulsion, you unlock ship designs for staying
  in subspace for prolonged periods, popping out, and popping back in.
  Y'know, like a submarine.  Unlike a normal ship, such ships have no subspace
  travel cooldown.  They can enter subspace more easily the farther they are
  from a star; closer in, they can enter subspace for far less time.  This
  implies they can arrive in a system, and then "cloak".  They must pop out of
  subspace to take a peek at the system they are in.  At even higher tech
  levels (including detection), they can see the system without leaving
  subspace, though this gives enemies information about their position; the
  more they use it, the more accurate their enemies' location estimates
  become.  At even higher levels (including weapons), they can attack from
  subspace; this gives away their location even more quickly.  Only very small
  ships can use this kind of subspace submarine technology.

* There are 50 tech levels for each tech area.  Each tech has a schedule of
  what techs are available at what level.  E.g. ship armor 1 at Construction
  3, ship armor 2 at Construction 6, etc.

* It should be easy to fall behind on tech, but hard to speed up tech
  progress.  Tech progress is based on sqrt(total pop in areas above a
  threshold I1 of infrastructure) .  This implies a certain amount of resource
  spending X.  Spending Y > X will increase the speed of progress to X *
  sqrt(Y / X), not Y.

* Rtotal RPs are generated for a given nation.  All these RPs are allocated
  among the tech areas.  The sum of all allocations is the Rtotal.  Not every
  area needs to be given RPs.  Assuming Na tech areas, each area gets a
  baseline of 1.0 / Na * private_research_factor, plus whatever is allocated
  from Rtotal.

* Evaluation of tech effects is typically partially adversarial.  For
  instance, the chance to detect a cloaked ship is based primarily on the
  ship's stealth tech level, but is modified by the difference between its
  stealth level and the detection levels of its adversaries.  As another
  example, a bonus damage to shields is given if an attacker has a higher
  weapons tech than the target's shield tech; a bonus damage to the ship
  itself is given if the attacker's weapon level is higher than the target's
  construction level.

* A given ship, base, or other unit may be unlocked when certain tech level
  minimums are met.  In other cases, capabilities are added to existing units.
  For instance, when fighters are unlocked, the first carriers are unlocked
  along with them; starbases get fighters at the same time (they may get them
  a bit at a time over subsequent tech levels).


Other:
======

* War support is a thing.  It can be manufactured in order to start a war, but
  when this is done it falls at the start of the war.  It rises dramatically
  at the start of a defensive war.

* War support increases when your ifra, pops, and/or ships (including
  transports) are attacked.

* War support increases when you lose territory -- up to some threshold
  percentage of your territory, T3.  After that, it falls precipitously.

* War support increases when you win battles, and decreases when you lose
  them.

* Low war support will force peace treaties that are unfavorable to the nation
  that has it.

* High war support dramatically reduces the likelihood of rebellions by the
  nation's settlements.  It does not affect rebellion likelihood of conquered
  territory.


* National unity is a thing.  National unity is affected by integration of
  conquered territory; the balance of near and far territories; and the
  balance of newer and more established settlements.

* Newer settlements have lower national unity than more established ones.  The
  smallest ones are the most independent.

* Settlement foundings and expansions increase national unity.

* Attacks on a settlement in times of peace reduce national unity.  Declaring
  war on those who have done such attacks greatly increases national unity.

* Distant planets have lower nationaly unity.  "Distant" here refers to their
  distance from large, established "core" settlements.  This changes over
  time, as the notion of what a "core" settlement is expands.  (TODO: Should
  this work sort of like supply, but measured among worlds -- that is, the
  effect is not in play as long as you can trace N-hex hops from loyal world
  to loyal world?)

* Recently conquered territories have low national unity.  Garrisons and
  fleets reduce this.  This fades over time.

* Settlements and provinces can secede, and settlements can do so
  independently of their containing province.  When a settlement or province
  drops below its loyalty threshold, it will secede.  The secession of a
  settlement will influence the loyalty of settlements within the same
  province, and the secession of a province will influence the loyalty of
  neighboring provinces.  This may result in a slow-motion secession of a
  large section of the empire.

* Infrastructure is a thing too.  You need to improve it to get the full value
  of resource generation, research, etc.  Infrastructure starts at 1 on a new
  settlement, and can be built up over time by spending money and other
  resources.  Max infra is limited by population.  Infra below 1 will cause
  population loss.  This is mitigated by stockpiles, but not entirely -- the
  infra is needed to get the stockpiles to those in need.


* The amount of available troops is dependent on the fighting-age population,
  which is basically the working-age population.  Base it loosely on the
  typical numbers from today:

  Metric              Typical Value (2026)    Description
  Working-Age Share   ~65%                    People aged 15–64.
  Youth Share         ~24-25%                 People aged 0–14.
  Elderly Share       ~10-11%                 People aged 65 and older.

  There should be some kind of settlement troop mobilization policy setting
  (like in HOI4), which will affect the number of available troops, and in
  turn affect the number of pops available to work on other things.  Troops
  are used to create divisions and to crew space assets (except transports).
  Troops take several months to be mobilized; this represents training time.

  The ratios above should be different in a large, developed settlement, vs. a
  fledgling settlement.

* Happiness/rebelliousness of a settlement should in part depend on how much
  funding they receive per capita vs. other settlements, their mobilization
  level vs. other settlements, etc.  This is based on how much they are
  above/below the mean.  The negative effect of being below the mean should be
  twice the positive effect of being above it.

* Making changes to policy should cost political points.  PPs should be
  generated indpendently for each settlement.  If you run out of PP in
  settlement A, you cannot make changes there, even if the capitol is swimming
  in PPs.  You should not be able to increase the amount of PPs through
  military action (sending troops and/or fleets), but you should be able to
  reduce the PP costs for taking actions by doing so.  The tradeoff is that
  this intimidation-based discount will cause future PP generation to
  decrease.  The mere presence of troops/ships is not enough; there must be a
  positive action taken to get the discount and the reduced PPs aftwerward.

* The cost in PPs of making a change should scale with the size of the change.
  If you want to make a certain choice when a decision event comes up, or if
  you want to change a settlement's governmental policy, you shoud pay more
  than the base PP cost if that is not how things are typically done, or if
  that is not in aligment with other current policies.  E.g., if a decision
  would increase militarism (as in the militarism vs. pacifism scale), is
  should be splightly cheaper in terms of PP if you are already militaristic,
  and should be more PP-expensive if you are on the pacifist side.  The
  farther you are from the center of the militarism vs. pacifism scale, the
  greater the effect.

* Making things costs money, resources, and pops to make (where the pops are
  doing the work, not being consumed).  Things you might make this way are:
  ships and bases, and other space assets; upgrading infrastructure;
  maintaining infrastructure; repairing infrastructure; rounds, missiles, and
  fighters for your fleets and bases to use; fuel (refining it -- what you
  pull out of the ground is not usable directly); and supplies.  It also takes
  money, resources, and pops to gather resources themselves.  Research is a
  similar story; it costs money resurces and pops to do generate RPs.
  Assigning all your pops is the main way you "spend" on making
  things/gathering resources/generating RPs.  Switching a pop from one task to
  another should incur a penalty on that pop's output that decays over time.

* There is a natural max number of pops needed/useful at doing a given
  activity.  Any less, and the output goes down linearly.  Any more, and the
  output goes up sublinearly, with a substantial penalty even for the first
  pop.  When all activities are fully staffed, the leftover pops will want to
  migrate to another settlement.  The amount of pops below refer to the pops
  in a certain settlement, not the whole nation.  Also, there is an implicit
  scale factor k for each of these, as game balance dictates.

  - The natural max for space asset construction scales linearly with shipyard
    capacity.

  - The natural max for upgrading infrastructure is sqrt(infra).

  - The natural max for maintaining infrastructure is infra.  This one is
    special, in that dipping below it causes damages to infrastructure.

  - The natural max for repairing infrastructure is sqrt(damage level), same
    as upgrading.

  - The natural max for a given resource R with a rating in [0, 100] is 100 /
    rating(R) / infra, *per rating point*, up to rating(R).  That is, it's
    inverse-linearly expensive to get a single point of R out of the ground,
    based in rating(R).  Note that this simplifies to just 100 / infra.

  - The natural max for manufacturing space consumables, refining fuel, and
    manufacturing supply is sqrt(infra).

  - The natural max for research is sqrt(pops) * ifra.

  - Population (in the form of mobilized troops) is also required for crewing
    space assets and for ground forces (garrisons).

  - Population is required for crewing transports, but these are civilians,
    not troops.

* Resources, space consumables (rounds, missiles, fighters), fuel, and
  supplies are all stockpiled when made.  The stockpile capacity of each
  planet scales with pop * infra.  Stockpiles can be moved among settlements
  via transports.  The amount that can be moved in/out is limited by
  infrastructure and the presence/capacity of orbital bases at the
  settlement's planet.

* Note that idle pops want to migrate, and where better to migrate to than
  that settlement right over there?  So, some pops might want to stay
  on-world, and go to another nation's settlement (especially if they are
  culturally and nationally similar, and on good terms).  This may cause
  friction if they are not 100% embraced by their new neighbors -- this should
  drive certain kinds of events.

* Some activities don't take resources and pops, just money.  Examples include
  funding diplomatic efforts and funding espionage/intelligence agencies.
  Such activities are fundable up to that max funding, and fundable beyond
  that, for diminishing returns (like with the activities already mentioned
  above).

  - Diplomatic efforts are expressed in terms of diplo capacity.  Diplo
    capacity should scale with sqrt(nation_pop).

  - Espionage/intelligence is also a capacity value, and also scales with
    sqrt(nation_pop).

  - Each settlement below S1 must be supported with money

  - Transports cost money to operate.

* Capacities like diplo and espionage can be allocated to different specific
  targets/activites.  Maintaining an alliance costs a certain amount,
  improving relations a different amount, etc.  Espionage activities can have
  more chance of success and less chance of detection the more you spend on
  them, and from longer preparation time (during which they are paid for at
  full cost).

* Money is generated from pop * infra, settlement by settlement.  Money is
  shared across the entire nation.  Settlements below a certain infra do not
  generate any money.  They will require money and supplies simply to survive.
  You can turn down the amount of funding (or amunt of supply) for a settlement,
  but that will result in loss of pop (for supplies), and lack of migration
  (for either).


War and Peace:
==============

* In order to declare war, a justification (cassus belli, "CB") is required.
  "No-CB" wars can be declared, but they are more negatively impactful
  diplomatically.  The better the CB, the less war support is needed to
  declare.  CBs include: attacks on settlements (cummulative -- the more there
  are, the stronger the CB), attacks on fleets (cummulative), settlements on
  planets/in systems where we have settled, TODO.

* Once war has started, a war score is computed for each side.  The war score
  for a side will include: strength of each side's fleet (including tech
  differential), captured and disrupted territory (friendly and enemy), war
  support, the length of the war, TODO.  The differential can be used to force
  concessions in a peace deal.  Each concession has a point value based on the
  game-value of that concession.  Possible concessions include: war
  reparations, worlds, provinces, TODO.


Nations and diplomacy:
======================

* Nations have these axes that desribe their ethos:

  - Individualist vs collectivist 
  - Nationalist vs inclusive 
  - Militarist vs pacifist 
  - Expansionist vs isolationist 
  - Order vs justice
  - Wealth equality vs wealth inequality

* Nations that are highly similar along these axes will be natural friends;
  those highly dissimilar will be natural enemies; and others will be neutral.

* These axes will also dictate AI behaviors, and will increase or decrease the
  PP costs of doing various things for the AI and player.

* Overall diplo system will be similar to Stellaris and EU4, except without
  the vassal/subject business (TODO: maybe with vassals too?)

* About relations: having very good relations for a long time should add
  something like a historical friends modifier.  Longstanding bad relations
  should add a historical enemies modifier.

* About diplo relations: There should be no limit on the number of alliances
  you have.  Most alliances should be defensive only.  Getting allies to join
  your war should require extended persuasion.  There should be like diplo
  points that you can spend to maintain good relations, persuade allies, etc.
  You should get these points from nation size primarily; this should cost
  resources and money.  You should also get them from soft power projects like
  usaid-style foreign aid, and allowing arms sales.

* TODO: Should there be some free points that you get, applied to just one
  nation, for helping that nation?  Like the arms sales above, or maybe from
  being historical friends/enemies?  Also, should enemies of the recipient be
  upset that you give arms to some country?  Should pacifists be upset too?
  Another idea would be to do something like the EU4 favor/trust system.

* Larger mutual defense pacts like NATO should be a thing.

* AE should be a thing, but it should cool off over the course of generations,
  and the decay should decrease as it gets lower (basically, it should have
  something like a half-life).

* Allies should remember not just that you joined their war, but how hard you
  fought.  Constantly white peacing out or not participating should be
  noticed.  These behaviors should affect relations, but also diplo rep.
  Diplo rep should be gained/lost over time.  It should be affected by war
  participation, truce breaks, etc.  It should also be improved over time by
  spending diplo points.  Should it also be improved by brokering peace deals
  among other parties?

* Military tradition is also a thing.  It should decay over time, modified by
  lengths of service for troops.  Militarist nations should have an easier
  time keeping tradition.

* Surpluses and deficits of resources will drive trade with other nations.

* Supply should benefit from planetary infra, and from space bases.  Having
  only one or the other should give lower supply capacity.

* Get list of contemporary names, first and last, from all over the world.
  Come up with some substitutions like t vs th, ae vs a, etc.  Use the
  substitutions to change the names, suggesting linguistic drift.  This is
  just for flavor.

* Consider generating flags.  Take two or three colors, make vertical or
  horizontal stripes, the add other stuff, to include circles, stars, squares,
  perhaps crosses and crescents too.


Government types:
=================

(A very early idea...)

There should be multiple government types, like: Oligarchic Dictatorship,
Monarchic Dictatorship, Presidential Republic, Perliamentary Republic, etc.

The more democratic ones would give you better (more reliable) information
about your own nation; the more dictatorial ones would give you a rosy picture
that is not very accurate.

The more democratic ones would require a lot more consensus building (red:
spend more PP) to do major, controversial things like start wars.  The more
dictatorial ones would let you do the same thing on the cheap.

The primary tradeof would be between direct control (dictatorships) and
quality and stability (democracies).  As a democratic nation, its harder to
start wars. and easier to get reliable info on your enemies.  As a
dictatorship, its easier to start wars, and also easier to get rebellions if
you demonstrate weakness.

Another way to describe this is ruling through strength vs. ruling through
consensus.


Factions:
=========

(Another early idea; this feels like a post-1.0 feature.)

It might be nice to have factions within your nation.  Each would have its
preferred government type(s), and have its own ethos alignment.


Visibility of Other Nations' Data
=================================

Visibility levels:
owner - I own this thing; I can see everything
allied - I'm allied to the owner of this thing; I can see most/all properties
known (enemy or neutral) - I have no relationship with the owner of this thing; I can see some properties
unseen - I don't even know this thing exists

All visibility levels besides unknown can see IDs; we won't keep repeating
those below.

A hex must be scouted to move from unknown to known.  When it is scouted, this
does not also scout the systems in the hex; those must be scouted individually
as well.

fleet: allied sees all; known sees just the units

unit: allied sees all; known sees only the design id and design owner

design: allied sees all; known sees only owner and hull (owner may or may not
be known); encounters with a certain design may fill in some/all of the
numbers

planet_effect: allied sees all; known sees only the permanent effects

planet: allied sees all; known sees only permanent things like planet_type
through ocean_coverage, the permanent effects part of growth_factor and
max_population, and a range of values for pop and infra (curr. owner too,
obvs.) intel is required to see about resources (this implies that planets
need to be sruveyed before you know what the resource availability is)

location_object: TODO

system_location: TODO, but in-system fleets should be harder to detect, due to
the star, stations and planets

star: everyone can see everything, once they've found the system it's in

system: allied sees all; known sees only name, coord, star,
permanent_locations, world_pos_*, *_planet

hex: everyone sees everything, once they have scouted the hex with an owned
ship (within the limitations above)

province: TODO

nation: allied see all; known cannot see unit designs it has not seen before,
except via intel(?), and canno tsee anything else (except "defeated") unless
they can scout it individually on the map, as described above.


Clandestine operations:
=======================
Could include things like:

- eliminating a small competing settlement without its being a war crime
  (super-lightspeed comms and settlement's friendlies in orbit would increase
  chances of detection)

- getting the resource amounts for a planet you have never been to, but which
  has been surveyed by someone else

- getting the resource stockpile amounts of another nation

- getting any other data from another nation that you normally cannot see
  (foreign designs, systems visited, etc.)

You should be able to spy on friendlies and enemies alike.  The possibility of
discovery and the diplomatic fallout from that should be an essential part of
this feature.

Stuff like stealing tech, destroying tech, stealing money, and blowing up
ships is too much.  It's always irritating in other games.


